Two distinct ways to get Jacobians out of a framework like this, and
they lead to different designs:

1. Autodiff Jacobians "for free" from the radiance-emulator you
already have. This is one of the standard selling points of NN-based
RT emulators in the literature (it comes up a lot in the RTTOV-NN and
related work) — once you have a differentiable network mapping inputs
→ radiance, dR/dInput is just backprop, no finite-differencing
needed. Your pipeline is well-suited to this because your
encode/decode steps are linear (PCA), so the chain rule is clean:

dR_channel/dT_level = [PCA_decoder matrix] × [dlgradient of NN w.r.t. input EOF coeffs] × [EOF_encoder matrix, with the log-derivative 1/Q folded in for gas variables]

In your custom training loop, this means looping over each of the
nPCA_BT output components (since dlgradient differentiates a scalar),
computing dlgradient(coeffOut(k), X) for each k, and chain-ruling
through the two linear compression steps by hand:

    function J = jacobianRadianceWrtInput(params, Xsample, iHidden, nPCA_BT)
        % Xsample: single profile, dlarray 'CB', (nFeatures x 1)
        J = zeros(nPCA_BT, size(Xsample,1));
        for k = 1:nPCA_BT
            [~, grads] = dlfeval(@(p,x) singleComponentGrad(p,x,iHidden,k), params, Xsample);
            J(k,:) = extractdata(grads)';
        end
    end
    
    function [y, g] = singleComponentGrad(params, X, iHidden, k)
        Yfull = forwardMLP(params, X, iHidden);
        y = Yfull(k);
        g = dlgradient(y, X);
    end

Then dR_channel/dcoeffs_in = btPCA.coeff * J gives you the
channel-by-input-coefficient Jacobian directly (since decode is
linear), and a further multiply by the T/Q/O3 EOF basis matrices (with
the 1/Q chain-rule factor for the log-transformed gas variables) gets
you back to level space if you want it there rather than in
EOF-coefficient space.

2. Train the network directly against (KCARTA − SARTA) Jacobian
residuals, using SARTA's own Jacobian as an input feature. This is
residual learning, same principle as the finetune_decoder discussion
from way back — instead of asking the network to learn RT physics from
scratch (which is exactly the gap we diagnosed as costing you the last
0.3-0.4 K on radiance), you hand it SARTA's already-close analytic
Jacobian as a baseline input/feature, and train it to predict only the
correction needed to match KCARTA truth. This should be a much easier
regression than the radiance problem you've been fighting, since the
residual is presumably small and smoother than the full Jacobian
itself.

These aren't mutually exclusive — you could use approach 1 to get the
emulator's own implied Jacobian as one input feature or as a
comparison baseline, while approach 2 is the actual training
target. But they do imply somewhat different architectures and I don't
want to guess wrong on which is closer to what you're after.

Which is closer to what you have in mind — get Jacobians as a
byproduct of a good radiance emulator (approach 1), or build a
dedicated residual-correction model specifically for SARTA Jacobians
against KCARTA truth (approach 2)? That'll determine whether we're
extending the current network or starting a parallel one with a
different input/output structure (SARTA's own Jacobian would need to
come in as an input either way, which is new).

