That's the tell, and it's a strong one: if a plain linear regression
has the same std-dev floor as the DNN, the ceiling isn't about model
expressivity at all — it's telling you the noise floor in your
training pairs is comparable to or larger than the physical signal
you're trying to resolve. No amount of network capacity fits real
information that isn't in the data at that precision. This is the same
lesson as the radiance case, just showing up even more starkly because
the signal itself (0.01 K) is so small relative to typical numerical
noise floors in Jacobian computation.

Given the constraint is 0.02 K noise on a 0.01 K signal, the most
likely culprits, roughly by probability:

1. Storage precision truncation. This is the first thing I'd check,
and it's the cheapest. If either KCARTA or SARTA radiances (or the
Jacobians themselves) get written to disk with limited precision —
single precision floats, or worse, ASCII with a fixed number of
decimal digits — then differencing two already-rounded numbers to
extract a 0.01 K-scale signal is asking for exactly this kind of
noise. Check bit-for-bit: what dtype/format are the saved
radiances/Jacobians in, and what's the effective precision after
round-trip through storage? A quick diagnostic: take a handful of
profiles, recompute the Jacobian difference directly in-memory without
any disk round-trip, and see if the std dev drops.

2. Finite-difference perturbation noise in whichever code computes
Jacobians via finite differencing rather than analytically. If
KCARTA's Jacobian is finite-difference (perturb T, rerun full LBL,
subtract, divide by δT), that's classic catastrophic-cancellation
territory when the true derivative is small — δT too small and
cancellation noise dominates, δT too large and truncation error
dominates, and the "sweet spot" isn't always in a comfortable place
for a signal this small. Test directly: recompute the KCARTA surftemp
Jacobian for a handful of profiles at two or three different δT values
(say half and double whatever you currently use) and look at how much
the resulting Jacobian itself moves. If it moves by ~0.01-0.02 K just
from changing δT, that's your answer — the "truth" you're regressing
against isn't stable at that scale, and no downstream model can
outperform the label noise it's trained on.

3. Which side is analytic vs. finite-difference matters a lot here. If
SARTA's Jacobian is computed analytically (I'd assume closer to this,
given the regression-coefficient formulation) and KCARTA's is
finite-difference, then essentially all the noise is coming from the
KCARTA side of the pair, and #2 is almost certainly the mechanism, not
#1.

Quick way to settle it: rerun the exact same profile's KCARTA Jacobian
twice — once through your existing pipeline (disk round-trip, existing
δT), once fully in-memory with a tightened/varied δT — and diff
them. If that alone reproduces ~0.02 K scatter, you've isolated it to
Jacobian-generation noise rather than anything downstream (network,
linear fit, feature engineering), and the DNN/regression framework is
essentially already at the ceiling the label noise allows — the fix is
in how the KCARTA (or SARTA) Jacobians are computed/stored, not in the
ML pipeline at all.

