That instinct is sound, and the SNR observation makes a strong case
for it — not just as "a fancier network," but because the specific
problem you've described (SARTA is constrained only at TOA, so its
internal layer-by-layer Jacobian structure is under-determined even
when its integrated output is excellent) is exactly the kind of
ill-posedness that a pure data-driven fit can't resolve on its own,
but a physics constraint can, in principle, resolve directly.

Why this is genuinely different from the radiance problem, not just a
bigger version of it:

For radiance, the target (TOA radiance) is a well-defined physical
quantity and the issue was information/representation. For
layer-by-layer Jacobians, you've identified something structurally
different: the "true" KCARTA−SARTA difference at any given layer isn't
uniquely determined by physics alone — because SARTA's regression
coefficients were only ever constrained to get the TOA sum right,
there can be multiple internally-different ways SARTA's layers could
combine to hit the same top-of-atmosphere answer. That means a plain
data-fit DNN is trying to learn a target that may not even be a clean,
smooth function of the input state — it could have some genuine
irreducible ambiguity baked in from how SARTA was fit. That's
consistent with what you're seeing: signal below or comparable to
noise, and neither linear nor DNN able to do better than the label
noise permits.

Where a PINN actually helps here, concretely: you know the exact
analytic relationship a physically correct set of layer Jacobians must
satisfy — the discretized Schwarzschild/adjoint recursion relating
dR_TOA/dT_layer_k to layer-to-space transmittance, the Planck
function's temperature derivative, and optical depth at each
level. That's not something you need to learn from noisy
finite-difference-style data at all — it's known, closed-form, and you
can compute it directly. So the PINN's physics loss term isn't
"encourage the network to vaguely respect radiative transfer" in the
abstract — it's a specific, checkable constraint: the network's
predicted layer-by-layer correction, when propagated through the known
transmittance recursion, must reproduce the correct (well-determined,
low-noise) TOA sensitivity. That's a genuine extra source of
information that doesn't depend on your noisy per-layer KCARTA-SARTA
differences at all — it's self-consistency, not more noisy labels —
and it directly targets the exact failure mode you described (locally
wrong, globally right).

Practically, the loss would look like:
Loss = w_data * (data-fit term, weighted down or masked where signal ≤ noise floor) + w_physics * ||recursion(predicted_layer_jacobians) − known_accurate_TOA_jacobian||^2

The physics term needs the adjoint/perturbation recursion implemented
as a differentiable function of the network's output (so gradients
flow back through it) — that's the real engineering lift, not
architecture, since it's mostly linear algebra you already know cold,
just written in dlarray so dlgradient can differentiate through it.

Given you're early in the DNN side of this specifically (even though
you're obviously not new to the physics), I'd suggest one gating step
before building the PINN machinery: rerun the signal-vs-noise check
across layers, not just surftemp. Surftemp is a boundary term and
might be an unusually hard case (small signal by nature). If
mid-tropospheric layer Jacobians show a healthier signal-to-noise
ratio, a data-only DNN might already work reasonably there, and the
PINN constraint becomes most valuable specifically in the regions
(like surftemp, or wherever else comes back noise-dominated) where
pure data fitting can't work no matter what.

Want to start by scoping that check across a few representative
