#1 — column-integrated absorber amount, not scaling the profile itself
Multiplying the raw per-level Q/O3 by secant(angle) before your
existing log-transform is actually close to a no-op information-wise:
log(Q * secant) = log(Q) + log(secant) — that's just a uniform
additive shift to the whole log-profile, and since view angle is
already a separate scalar input, the network can already reconstruct
that shift on its own (assuming it can learn that simple a
relationship, which it should). So doing it that way probably buys you
very little.

What I actually mean is different: compute column-integrated absorber
amount — a genuinely different quantity than any per-level value, and
not recoverable by simply rescaling the profile. This is a cumulative
sum down through the atmosphere, and it's the quantity that directly
determines transmittance/optical depth at a channel — level-by-level Q
values (or their EOF compression) require the network to effectively
re-derive this integral itself from 15 separately-compressed shape
coefficients, which is a much harder implicit task than just handing
it the integral directly.

    % precipitable water column, TOA down to surface, then slant-path scaled
    g = 9.80665;
    % Q here should be specific humidity/mixing ratio at native pressure levels
    columnWaterVapor_i = trapz(pressure_i, Q_i) / g;      % kg/m^2, or convert to mm
    slantColumnWV_i    = columnWaterVapor_i * secant_i;

    % same idea for ozone -> Dobson units, then slant-scaled
    columnO3_i      = trapz(pressure_i, O3_i) / g * DU_conversion_factor;
    slantColumnO3_i = columnO3_i * secant_i;

Even better, given you know exactly which channels are sensitive to
which altitude ranges from your remote-sensing experience: compute
partial columns — cumulative absorber amount from TOA down to a
handful of reference pressures (say 850, 500, 300, 100, 30 hPa) —
rather than just one whole-column number. That directly hands the
network the "how much absorber sits above this level" quantity that
governs each channel's weighting function, which is exactly the kind
of predictor SARTA-style regressions are built from. A handful of
these (5-6 per gas) alongside your existing EOF coefficients, not
replacing them.

#2 — T×Q coupling, made explicit rather than implicit

Right now T and Q go through separate PCA compressions (tPCA, qPCA),
so their EOF coefficients are two independent sets of numbers — any
physical coupling between temperature and absorption strength has to
be reconstructed by the network from two disjoint sets of coefficients
that were never told they interact. The cheapest fix: before
compressing, form the elementwise product profile and compress that
too, as an extra input:

    TQ_interaction = T .* Q;      % elementwise, per level, per profile -- (nSamples x nLevels)
    tqPCA = fitSpectralPCA(TQ_interaction, nPCA_TQ);   % nPCA_TQ can be small, e.g. 5-8
    tqEOF = encodeSpectral(tqPCA, TQ_interaction, true);

Add tqEOF alongside tEOF/qEOF in your feature matrix X. This isn't the
rigorous line-strength temperature dependence (which really involves
the HITRAN temperature-exponent term, more like a
Boltzmann/exp(-E''/kT) relationship), but it's a cheap, honest
first-order proxy for the coupling, and given your time constraint
it's a much smaller lift than implementing the exact spectroscopic
temperature dependence per gas.

