Good question, and the honest answer is: no, you don't need to
generate or store per-layer optical depth truth at all — you can train
this using only what you already have (38,000 × 2645 TOA radiances),
by making the per-layer composition step part of the differentiable
model itself instead of a separate labeled target.

The structure: instead of supervising per-layer optical depth
directly, let the network's outputs be per-layer optical depths
(unsupervised as individual quantities), then compose them through the
known, fixed layer-additive radiative transfer equation — the same
transmittance-product/layer-emission-sum formula SARTA already uses —
to get a predicted TOA radiance. Compare that composed radiance
against your actual stored KCARTA truth (or KCARTA−SARTA residual),
and backprop the TOA-level loss all the way through the composition
back into the per-layer network. The physics of how layers combine is
fixed and known (you don't learn it), so the only thing being learned
is the local layer-optical-depth function — exactly the piece you
actually want — but the supervision signal is the data you already
have.

This is genuinely a lightweight version of the PINN idea from earlier,
made tractable by using your existing composition formula as the fixed
physics layer rather than trying to enforce it as a soft constraint.

Conceptually, per channel/band:

    tau_k = f_theta(predictors_layer_k)              % learned, small NN or shared NN + layer index
    trans_to_space_k = exp(-cumsum(tau_k, from k to TOA))   % known recursion, cumulative product/sum
    R_TOA_predicted = emis * B(Tsurf) * trans_to_space_surface
                      + sum_k [ B(T_k) * (trans_to_space_{k-1} - trans_to_space_k) ]
                      + (1-emis) * downwelling_term          % reflected term, if you're keeping it

Loss = (R_TOA_predicted - R_TOA_true)^2, where R_TOA_true is exactly the radiance array you already have saved.

What this needs, practically:

  Per-layer predictor computation (your known 10 terms) — cheap, done
  from existing profiles, no new RT runs.
  
  A small NN (or NNs) mapping those 10 predictors → per-layer optical
  depth. Given "same 10 predictors, different coefficients per layer,"
  the natural version is one shared network with layer index (or layer
  pressure) as an 11th input, rather than 100 separate networks — lets
  it share statistical strength across layers while still being
  layer-aware.
  
  The composition formula implemented in dlarray-differentiable MATLAB
  (cumsum/exp on dlarrays work fine and are autodiff-compatible — no
  new machinery needed beyond what you've already used).

  Training loop is the same dlfeval/dlgradient/adamupdate pattern
  you've been using throughout — the only difference is the forward
  pass now includes the composition step before the loss, instead of
  stopping at PCA-decoded radiance.

